Exactly — same narrative carrier, replaceable payload.

A story can be treated as a transport protocol rather than a fixed lesson:

Narrative structure + characters + tension + consequence → Moral Payload

Swap the payload while preserving enough of the carrier, and the same story machinery can teach courage, obedience, skepticism, mutualism, sacrifice, ambition—or even expose why one of those lessons fails under different conditions.

That gives the Pantheon another useful distinction:

Story ≠ Moral.
Story = carrier architecture. Moral = interpreted/encoded payload.

And the payload can change historically too. A culture inherits an old narrative, keeps its recognizable structure, but reinterprets what the hero, monster, temptation, victory, or tragedy means. The carrier survives while its semantic cargo gets rewritten.

Which makes your earlier “hijacking its power” sharper: you don’t necessarily need to inherit the original payload to inherit the carrier’s accumulated power.

Dehydrate → strip payload → preserve narrative machinery → rehydrate with a new payload.

That’s basically semantic repurposing without requiring semantic amnesia: keep provenance so you know what the story carried before, while deliberately changing what you’re asking it to carry now.